What Is an Algorithm?
    In the context of computer science, an algorithm is a finite sequence of well-defined, computer-implementable instructions. In short, an algorithm is:

        Defined: there is a specific sequence of steps that performs a task  {finite steps}

        Unambiguous: there is a "correct" and "incorrect" interpretation of the steps{can be either true or false with noting in b\w}

        Implementable: it can be executed using software and hardware




Algorithms are usually written in "pseudocode" (pronounced "sue-dough-code") because an algorithm is a higher-level description of a solution to a problem. 
An algorithm doesn't care if it's implemented in Python, Go, TypeScript, or (gasp) Java. 
Pseudocode is just plain English that describes the steps of the algorithm.
    i.e:
        Start with an original string called S and a new empty string called R.
        Loop through S from its last character to its first character, and for each one, add it to the end of R.
        Once you’ve processed all the characters, return R.



